  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Louisiana federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date total $5.2B across 22,577 awards, with an average award size of $231K. Obligations are up 9.8% year over year. Department of Homeland Security and Department of Defense lead buying, obligating $2.5B and $1.9B respectively. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry by obligations at $2.7B, reflecting major shipbuilding and repair work in the state. BOLLINGER SHIPYARDS LOCKPORT, L.L.C. is the largest vendor with $2.3B in obligations, followed by STRATEGIC STORAGE PARTNERS, LLC. at $430M and PITTMAN-SEALEVEL, L.L.C. at $420M. Other notable sectors include Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $479M and Facilities Support Services at $434M. The FY21–FY26 trend shows FY26 at $5.2B after FY25 at $4.8B and FY24 at $3.9B, indicating recent growth in obligations. This snapshot covers fiscal year to date totals and reflects contract obligations only.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove FY2026 spending in Louisiana?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security drove a large share of spending, obligating $2.5B. The Department of Defense is also a major buyer, obligating $1.9B. Ship Building and Repairing dominated industry obligations at $2.7B, concentrating much of the state’s contract dollars."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which vendors and industries dominate FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "BOLLINGER SHIPYARDS LOCKPORT, L.L.C. is the top vendor with $2.3B in obligations. STRATEGIC STORAGE PARTNERS, LLC. and PITTMAN-SEALEVEL, L.L.C. follow with $430M and $420M respectively. Ship Building and Repairing leads industries at $2.7B, with Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction and Facilities Support Services at $479M and $434M."
      }
    ]
  },
